  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-01-02T07:10:46.014Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "As it stands, Quest content creators have no means of testing content on their own. These means that content may frequently have to be uploaded for quest testing purposes. \n \nProposal: \nProvide as part of the VRCSDK a \"(Localhost) Quest Testing Server Utility\". This lightweight utility would function as a limited-form locally-hosted VRChat server, capable of \"hosting\" Quest-compatible content on the local network (LAN) using your computer's LAN IP and a specified port. \n \nOnce started, the \"QTSU\" would provide status information, and a button with the option to \"send invite\" to the SDK-linked account. \n \nOnce signed in, Quest devices connected to the same network, permitting a compatible network environment, would then be able to accept this invite, permitting \"untrusted\" URLs have been enabled, and connect to the local server running on the developer machine. \n \nQuest devices \"off network\" would not be capable of connecting to this server. \n \nFor world testing, this will serve the tested world, as desired. \n \nFor avatars, a provided \"avatar testing room\" will be hosted, with a pedestal for selecting the testing-avatar. Profile thumbnail and avatar details shall not update server-side whilst utilizing testing-avatars, and upon leaving the world, the user's avatar shall revert to their last known selection.\n \nExample: \n\"Johnny\" is a Quest content creator. Their development machine has an IP of 127.0.0.10, and port 1234 is open to the QTSU. Johnny builds \"My first world\", and runs the QTSU from the VRCSDK. Johnny then launches VRChat on their Quest 2, and sends a self-invite using the QTSU control panel. Their Quest 2 is connected to the same network, with an IP of 127.0.0.11, and they successfully join their world on the testing server at 127.0.0.10:1234. Once they are finished testing, they exit the world and close the QTSU.",
